think Round-open clean at 7ded4f2cd9, five standing claims all stale, no overlap; opened and held for the cold run.
think Cold run closed tree_moved=no, 364 green, 14 red, 3 gated -- run_verdict=guard_red is the roster's own expected self-check.
think Surveyed the 14 reds for something small and fixable: falsifier_form_outcome, shim_reason, ignored_walk, rye_compiled_reach, and comment_path are each a population wanting a larger plan or Keaton's word; width_check, standing_equipment_redleg, query_wire_retention, ceiling_teeth, shared_build_path, build_target were already named untriaged by prior laps; rule_twin and pond_enclosure_* stay gated.
think stash_record read unlanded=7, naming seven dated session logs that stand in a round-open stash and on no ref, no branch, no worktree -- confirmed each absent from every ref with git log --oneline --all.
think Extracted each from its stash's untracked-files parent (stash@{N}^3, the third parent git stash --include-untracked creates), not stash@{N} itself -- the first attempt against the bare ref failed with "exists on disk, but not in stash@{N}" until I read the parent structure.
think All seven read as valid session-log-v1 records with real title/obs fields from the laps that wrote them; none overlaps any currently-tracked file.
think This tree's own 20260925 shelf already documents this exact recovery pattern many times over ("Parked bounds witness recovered", "Grass packet recovered", "Recovered bound type roster") -- landing a stashed log by writing the file and adding its shelf row is standing practice here, including into an already-closed shelf, since the shelf's own count line is meant to track true content rather than freeze at whatever a prior lap happened to see.
think Wrote all seven files into session-logs/date/20260925/ and session-logs/date/20260926/, then inserted one shelf-qualified index row per log at its correct chronological position (newest-first) on the correct day shelf, updating 20260926's closed-count banner from 30 to 36 rows and naming the REDS stamp of this repair.
think First draft of the six new rows on the 20260926 shelf ran 182-223 bytes against the 192-byte row bound; shortened the bracket text and swapped "Recovered from stash." for "stash-recovered." to bring every row under the bound (134-185 bytes final).
obs No REDS row booked (REDS.md had room) -- this is an ordinary unrostered repair, matching stash_record's own named cure, overlapping no live claim.
obs Ran the scan directly first to confirm unlanded fell 7 to 0 before touching the witness; then ran ascii_document_scan and tame_style_check on the touched files, both GREEN.
obs Chose not to pop or drop any of the seven stashes -- several (stash@{2}, stash@{3}) hold dozens of other paths that are unrelated leftover working-tree state from old round-opens, and popping would reintroduce them uninvited. Extracting one blob via git show leaves the stash entries themselves untouched, which is the safer read-only half of the recovery.
